# The native shell's local environment — the committed TEMPLATE. Copy it to
# `native/.env` (gitignored, and kept out of the EAS upload by .easignore) and
# fill in what you need; every value is optional for a local build.
#
# NOTHING HERE IS READ BY THE EAS CLI FROM THIS FILE — it reads the PROCESS
# environment. Export the values (or set them as CI secrets/variables) before
# `eas build`; a shell that merely has a `.env` beside it has exported nothing.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EAS. Only needed to drive builds non-interactively (CI does this with the
# EXPO_TOKEN repo secret); a laptop uses `eas login` instead.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# An Expo access token. Authenticates the EAS CLI with no interactive login,
# and takes precedence over any `eas login` session on the same machine.
#
# WHERE: https://expo.dev/settings/access-tokens → **Create token** → copy it
# (shown once). A ROBOT token (https://expo.dev/accounts/<account>/settings/
# access-tokens) is the right kind for CI: it cannot sign in to the dashboard
# and owns no projects, so its blast radius is bounded. The GitHub workflow
# that drives EAS (.github/workflows/native.yml, manual dispatch only) reads it
# from the `EXPO_TOKEN` repo secret, so on CI it is set there rather than here.
# SHAPE: an opaque string, no fixed prefix. Treat it as a password.
# EXPO_TOKEN=

# The Expo project this app builds under, until it is pinned in app.config.js.
#
# WHERE: `eas init` creates the project and prints its id (also on the project
# page at https://expo.dev/accounts/<account>/projects/<slug>). Once the
# project exists, pin the id in app.config.js (`EAS_PROJECT_ID`) so nobody
# needs this variable; CI reads it from the `EAS_PROJECT_ID` repo variable
# until then.
# SHAPE: a lowercase UUID, 8-4-4-4-12.
# EAS_PROJECT_ID=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local iPhone builds (`make native-iphone`). Not needed for the simulator,
# and never for EAS, which signs with the credentials on the Expo project.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The Apple Developer team a local device build signs with. Every contributor
# signs with their own, which is why it is not committed.
#
# WHERE: https://developer.apple.com/account → **Membership details** → Team
# ID. Locally it is also the parenthesised code in Xcode → Settings → Accounts
# beside the team name, and in `security find-identity -v -p codesigning`.
# SHAPE: ten upper-case alphanumerics, e.g. ABCDE12345. Not a secret — it is
# an identifier, not a credential — but it is a PERSONAL detail, so it stays
# out of the repository.
# APPLE_TEAM_ID=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SHIPPING THE LISTING. What `fastlane deliver`, `eas submit` and
# `make store-preflight` authenticate and identify themselves with. None of it
# is needed to build or run the app; all of it is needed to submit one.
#
# A value still equal to the one in THIS file counts as unset (see
# scripts/lib/store-env.mjs), so a copied template does not read as filled in.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# THE NUMBER APPLE RINGS. App Store review calls the contact on the listing, so
# this has to be a line somebody answers — which is exactly why it is here and
# not in native/store/listing.mts: that file is committed to a PUBLIC
# repository, and a phone number in it is published permanently and in the
# history. The generator leaves the field out of the upload rather than hand
# review a number that rings nobody, and `make store-preflight` fails until
# this is set.
# SHAPE: E.164 with the country code, e.g. +46701234567. The + is required.
# ASC_REVIEW_PHONE=

# THE APP STORE CONNECT API KEY — three values, one credential. An API key
# rather than an Apple ID because a .p8 carries no 2FA session to expire in the
# middle of a forty-minute upload.
#
# WHERE: App Store Connect → Users and Access → Integrations → App Store
# Connect API → generate a key with the App Manager role. The .p8 downloads
# EXACTLY ONCE; keep it out of git (native/*.p8 is gitignored).
# SHAPE: ASC_KEY_ID is ten alphanumerics (the id in the AuthKey_<ID>.p8
# filename); ASC_ISSUER_ID is a lowercase UUID, one per team, shown above the
# key list; ASC_KEY_PATH is a path RELATIVE TO native/, because that is where
# the fastlane lanes run.
# ASC_KEY_ID=
# ASC_ISSUER_ID=
# ASC_KEY_PATH=AuthKey_XXXXXXXXXX.p8

# ...or the same key as base64, for CI, where there is no file to point at.
# Set exactly ONE of these two: with both set, the loser is silently ignored.
# WHERE: `base64 -i AuthKey_XXXXXXXXXX.p8` (macOS) / `base64 -w0` (Linux).
# ASC_KEY_CONTENT=

# The Google Play service-account JSON `eas submit` uploads an AAB with.
# WHERE: Play Console → Setup → API access → a service account with the
# Release Manager role, then its key from the Google Cloud console.
# SHAPE: a path to the .json, relative to native/. Gitignored like the .p8.
# PLAY_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_PATH=play-service-account.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build-time escape hatch. For LOCAL builds only — a store build must not set
# it. Left commented out on purpose. See docs/configuration.md.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Points the WebView at a deployed slot instead of the copy of the game bundled
# inside the app. Debugging only: a store build that streams the website is the
# exact shape App Store guideline 4.2 rejects.
# VALUE: any URL the device can reach — a deploy slot on the live origin, or
# your dev server on the LAN (http://<your-lan-ip>:5173/, not localhost, which
# on a phone means the phone).
# EXPO_PUBLIC_GAME_URL=https://game2.niclaslindstedt.se/preview/
